---
// What the tour simulator takes from the game and what it assumes (static; ships no JavaScript).
---

<section class="tour-notes" aria-label="About the simulation">
  <p>
    <b>Straight from the game files:</b> the three backers and their 18 contracts with credits per unit, form scoring (points per finishing
    position and per won bet), the bottom 3 being labelled Underdog and the Underdog bonus, the six planets and their order, each planet's
    shop and reward odds by rarity, every price (parts, abilities, upgrades, packs, rerolls), credits per race and placing, the three risk
    bets, shop size (4 parts), the 33.3% chance a part comes from a family you already own, and Multiplayer Tour EXP: per finishing
    position, per won risk bet, for a top-three tour finish, and for the final race bet.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Assumed, because the game keeps it in code:</b> how many options each reward or pack shows, how much a race reward upgrade adds (a bought Upgrade Pack always adds 1), that reroll
    prices reset at each shop, that a contract pays its rate for every unit you achieve (targets are shown as the game's starting goal), and
    the other 11 racers: each gets a hidden strength at the start of the tour and finishes by that strength plus a little variation each
    race. If the game labels you differently, use the Underdog switch on the bet screen. Change the first two under "Assumptions" when you
    start a run.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Backers:</b> at the first race each of the three backers offers one contract. The backer you pick stays with you for the whole tour,
    and at every later race but the last it offers three of its own contracts to choose from.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>The final race:</b> its winner wins the tour. There's no contract before it and no rewards or shop after it. Instead of a risk bet you
    back the racer you think will win, for EXP. Every player who backs the same racer shares it: the fewer, the more each gets, so enter how
    many backed your pick. A 2nd place pays half and a 3rd a quarter. Tour EXP leaves out win and participation streaks, which carry over
    between tours.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Parts:</b> you hold one rarity of each part. Lower rarities of a part you own are never offered; a higher rarity can be, and taking it
    replaces the one you own in the same slot.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Recommendations:</b> every part, ability and trait is tagged from its game rules: what it triggers on and what it gives. An offer
    scores higher when it shares your build's focus, gives something your build triggers on, or triggers on something your build produces.
    Upgrades follow your racer style's own upgrade priority from the game files. It's a guide, not a solver.
  </p>
</section>

